/*! \page developer_backend_page Supporting Multiple Backends

\ref developer_container_page (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_test_page      (Next)
<BR>

The PSCF package can builds two executable files, named pscf_rpc and 
pscf_rpg, that are variants of the pscf_rp program designed to use
different hardware.  The pscf_rpg executable makes use of a GPU, while 
the pscf_rpg executable uses standard CPU hardware.

The differences between two programs arise primarily from differences in:

   - Where are large arrays of data stored (in CPU vs. GPU memory) ?

   - Where and how are mathematical operations on such arrays performed 
     (on the CPU or GPU)?

These differences are encapsulated within the implementation of a limited
number of class templates that implement the relevant data structures and
algorithms for basic mathematical operations.

We summarize these differences in what follows by saying that these two
versions of pscf_rp use different computational "backends".  In the 
pscf_rpg executable, which uses a Cuda backend, almost all large arrays 
are allocated in GPU global memory, and basic mathematical operations 
that are performed on these arrays are performed on the GPU within 
CUDA kernels.  In the pscf_rpc executable, which uses a C++ backend, all 
data structures that use heap memory are allocated in CPU main memory, 
and all computations are performed on the CPU using code written in 
standard C++.

The system used by PSCF to support the use of more than one backend is 
based on the use of C++ class templates to minimize code duplication. 
The subpages linked below describe this system.

